Not a literal unified diff (scheduler_tick.cpp is a runtime-authored file with
no pristine baseline to diff against). This documents, for the record, the
starvation-detection addition made directly to
lib/N64ModernRuntime/ultramodern/src/scheduler_tick.cpp during the 2026-08-22
macOS boot investigation (see MACOS_BOOT_HANDOFF.md).

Problem: the existing voluntary-preemption monitor only detects "no context
switch into any game thread for kStuckThresholdMs (200ms)". That misses the
starvation case actually hit during Stadium 2 boot: several higher-priority
threads (100/90/80) keep trading control among *themselves*, so
g_last_switch_ms keeps advancing and the monitor never fires, while a lower
-priority thread ("Thread5", priority 40) sits at the head of running_queue
the entire time and never gets a turn. ~150ms after being queued, unrelated
code running on one of the higher-priority threads happens to write into
memory that is still Thread5's un-run OSThread struct (a real, ROM-accurate
hardcoded guest address — not a recompiler bug), corrupting it before it ever
ran.

Fix: track how long the SAME thread has sat at the head of running_queue
(via the already-exported `ultramodern_running_queue_head()`), independent of
whether switches are happening elsewhere, and fold that into the same
g_should_yield flag / yield_to_any_queued() delivery path the existing
"stuck thread" check already uses. Uses a much tighter threshold
(kStarvationThresholdMs = 40ms vs. the 200ms stuck threshold) and a faster
monitor poll (10ms vs. 50ms), since a queued-but-never-run thread is abnormal
much sooner than a genuinely-stuck busy loop, and the corrupting write in
this case has been observed within ~150ms of the thread being queued.

Status: this DID NOT fully resolve the Stadium 2 boot stall — the corrupting
write happens essentially immediately (same/adjacent instruction stream)
after Thread5 is queued, faster than any host-thread monitor polling
interval can reliably win the race. It is left in place as a genuine,
low-risk improvement to general scheduler fairness (any other starvation
scenario with a >40ms gap will now self-correct), but the Stadium 2 stall
itself needs a different fix — see MACOS_BOOT_HANDOFF.md's "Current
blocker" section for the precise call chain and next-step guidance.

Changes made to scheduler_tick.cpp:

1. Added `extern "C" uint32_t ultramodern_running_queue_head(void);` forward
   declaration (already defined in threadqueue.cpp).

2. Added two new atomics next to g_last_switch_ms:
     std::atomic<uint32_t> g_run_queue_head_seen{0};
     std::atomic<uint64_t> g_run_queue_head_since_ms{0};

3. Added `constexpr uint64_t kStarvationThresholdMs = 40;` next to
   kStuckThresholdMs, and reduced kMonitorIntervalMs from 50 to 10.

4. In monitor_func(), before the existing "is anything stuck" check, added:
     uint32_t head = ultramodern_running_queue_head();
     if (head == 0) {
         g_run_queue_head_seen.store(0, std::memory_order_relaxed);
         g_run_queue_head_since_ms.store(0, std::memory_order_relaxed);
     } else if (head != g_run_queue_head_seen.load(std::memory_order_relaxed)) {
         g_run_queue_head_seen.store(head, std::memory_order_relaxed);
         g_run_queue_head_since_ms.store(now, std::memory_order_relaxed);
     } else {
         uint64_t since = g_run_queue_head_since_ms.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
         if (since != 0 && now - since > kStarvationThresholdMs) {
             g_should_yield.store(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
             continue;
         }
     }
   (the existing last-switch check follows unchanged after this block)
